|> Exercise
    handle=_archimedean_projection_new_ver_

    By what factor are areas stretched
    as a function of the latitude $\theta$ in the
    Lambert projection of a sphere onto a cylinder?
    (Cf. Example >>_secant_example_.)
    For reference, a profile view of the Lambert projection
    is also given below.

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch5_ex_horizontal_projection_sphere_statement.svg

    |> Solution

        Areas are scaled by a factor of
        $$
        \sec(\theta) \cdot \cos(\theta) = 1
        $$
        because latitudes are stretched by a factor of

        $$
        \sec(\theta)
        $$

        !! |> ImageRight
        !!     offset_x=9em
        !!     src=images/svg_ch5_ex_horizontal_projection_sphere_sec_cloud.svg

        !! by the projection,
        as covered in Example >>_secant_example_,
        whereas longitudes (the other
        ones, perpendicular to latitudes) are locally stretched
        by a factor of...

        $$
        \cos(\theta)
        $$

        ...as depicted in this...

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_horizontal_projection_sphere_cos_explanation.svg

        ...sketch, and because the
        overall scaling in area is the latitudinal
        scaling factor times the longitudinal
        scaling factor. (Area = width $\times$ height,
        so you take the factor by which “width” scales,
        times the factor by which “height” scales—presuming
        your old “width” and “height” are still at right
        angle to one another after the projection, which
        is the case here: longitudes and latitudes, that
        are perpendicular on the sphere, project to
        lines and circles, respectively, that are
        perpendicular on the cylinder.)

        |> SolutionNote
            As a consequence, 
            the area of a shape on the sphere is equal to
            the area of the projection of that shape onto
            the cylinder, for any shape.
            We will refer to this (strange?) fact as the

            __Archimedean property__

            of the sphere.
            !! of as the “area-preserving property of the Lambert
            !! projection”, which is more self-explanatory.

            !! This 
            !! !! (strange?) 
            !! !! fact 
            !! is known as the

            !! __Archimedean property of the sphere__
            !! !! though we will refer to it 
            !! or as the

            !! __area-preserving property of the
            !! Lambert projection__

            !! !! for shortness.
            !! !! maybe-possibly.
            !! !! as would be self-explanatory.
            !! !! ...but which is uninterestingly self-explanatory.
            !! with the latter being more self-explanatory, but
            !! which, alas, gives credit to someone who is not a
            !! mathematician! (And very much not the first to discover it,
            !! but that is a lesser sin.)

        |> SolutionNote
            For example, the following two orange areas, one
            on the sphere and one on the cylinder, are
            equal, by the Archimedean property (this is a Lambert
            projection):

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch5_ex_horizontal_projection_sphere_orange_1.svg

            For a more fun example,
            the 8 “tube sections” 
            of each circle
            in this diagram...

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch5_ex_horizontal_projection_sphere_orange_9.svg

            ...have equal areas on the cylinder,
            even though dissimilar in shape,
            since those sections
            have equal area to one another on the sphere.
            (For each circle separately,
            we mean.)

            !! For a more fun example, 
            !! since all corresponding areas
            !! on the sphere and on the cylinder are equal in
            !! this example by the Archimedean property...

            !! |> Image
            !!     src=images/svg_ch5_ex_horizontal_projection_sphere_orange_9.svg

            !! ...it implies that the various “tube sections”
            !! of either circle have equal areas on the cylinder,
            !! even though dissimilar in shape, since those sections
            !! have equal area to one another on the sphere!
            !! (For each circle separately.)
